  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 218 PM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Patchy fog and low stratus will build over the \nregion early Wednesday, slowly lifting during the afternoon hours \ntomorrow.\n\nA backdoor cold front is presently located to our north in the \nDelmarva area and off the northern Mid-Atlantic coast. This front is \nforecast to approach overnight tonight and early tomorrow morning. \nWhile the actual front is not likely to move through, it will lead \nto pooling of moisture in the low-levels. The northeast moist low-\nlevel flow will lead to increasing surface dewpoints into the 50s \nalong and east of the Triangle by sunrise Wednesday. \n\nGuidance is pretty consistent on a layer of low stratus spreading \nwest from the Coastal Plain into the Piedmont during the overnight \nand early morning hours. With dewpoints also increasing, patchy fog \nwill also be possible, especially in outlying areas where winds go \ncalm. This layer of low stratus is expected to gradually lift and \ndisperse during the mid to late afternoon Wednesday. Highs tomorrow \nwill be a touch cooler than today as a result of the cloud cover and \nslightly lower thicknesses, generally upper 60s west to low/mid 70s \nin the east.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... High pressure will keep dry and near to above \nnormal temperatures through Saturday. A chance of rain remains \nSunday into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure ridging will continue to bring seasonal to above \naverage temperatures through Saturday. Our warmest day is expected \nto be Friday, ahead of another backdoor front that slides through \nearly Saturday. Highs on Friday could be some 10-12 degrees above \nnormal in the upper 70s to around the low 80s. \n\nThe backdoor front is forecast in much of the guidance to move \nthrough early Saturday, with cool 1030 to 1034-mb high pressure \nextending down into the region into early next week. This should \nfavor highs generally in the upper 60s to middle 70s.\n\nWe continue to monitor the chance of rain late Sunday afternoon into \nmidday Monday, with perhaps another chance by the middle to latter \npart of next week. But focusing on the Sun-Mon period, there remains \nstill a bit of ensemble spread. Specifically, the machine-learning \nensemble guidance continues to be on the dry side, as a result of a \nmore flat and muted mid-level shortwave, thereby limiting isentropic \nascent. Some wetter ensemble solutions show a more developed wave \ntracking through and thus better rain chances. But even given that, \nthe spread in rainfall is still large, ranging from just a trace to \nthree quarters of an inch. Thus, confidence remains low on expected \nrainfall, if any, at this stage.",
